--TEST--
An import binds one whole short name and is never a namespace prefix, so `use Core\Json;` followed by `#[Json\Derive]` resolves to nothing and is refused where it is written, while the whole name and the short name a `use` of it binds both reach the same recognized attribute (`rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/the-names-the-compiler-acts-on
// `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`: a recognized name is matched after it resolves, and an
// import binds one whole short name rather than a namespace prefix. The two
// accepted spellings are written first — the whole name, and the short name a
// `use` of that whole name binds — so a build that stopped resolving either
// fails here rather than as a missing refusal. `use Core\Json;` followed by
// `#[Json\Derive]` therefore names nothing at all, and is refused where it is
// written instead of attaching an inert literal nothing ever reads.
use Core\Json;
use Core\Json\Derive;

#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Long {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

#[Derive]
class Short {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

#[Json\Derive]
class Prefixed {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

echo Json::encode(new Long("ada")), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0303]: `Json\Derive` is not declared
   --> case.nvs:25:3
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
